18:03:16 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for Summary with this data : {'agent_id': 'Summary', 'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'message': '## Comparison\n\n| Aspect | Official Python documentation | Real Python |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Authoritative reference for Python’s syntax and semantics | Tutorial designed to build conceptual understanding and practical skill |\n| **Definition** | Treats a decorator as an expression that transforms a function or class during definition | Describes a decorator as a function that wraps another function to modify or extend its behavior |\n| **Core equivalence** | `@dec` is formally equivalent to applying the decorator after the definition: `func = dec(func)` | Demonstrates the same equivalence through runnable examples such as `say_whee = decorator(say_whee)` |\n| **Level of detail** | Concise and technical | Progressive, explanatory, and example-heavy |\n| **Main topics** | Grammar, evaluation, function/class definitions, and language behavior | First-class functions, nested functions, closures, wrappers, arguments, return values, metadata, stacking, and use cases |\n| **Examples** | Minimal examples intended to clarify the specification | Practical examples involving logging, timing, caching, authentication, plugin registration, and more |\n| **Decorator arguments** | Documents the syntax and rules but does not primarily teach implementation patterns | Explains decorators that accept arguments and how to structure them |\n| **Multiple decorators** | Specifies the language behavior | Explains stacking and shows why decorator order affects the result |\n| **Audience** | Developers seeking an authoritative reference | Beginners and intermediate programmers learning how decorators work |\n\n## What the official documentation emphasizes\n\nThe Python documentation focuses on what decorators mean as a language feature. Its glossary explains that decorator syntax is syntactic sugar:\n\n```python\n@dec\ndef func():\n    pass\n```\n\nhas the same effect as:\n\n```python\ndef func():\n    pass\n\nfunc = dec(func)\n```\n\nThe language reference is the better source for precise questions about syntax, evaluation order, and the guarantees made by Python. It also covers decorators applied to class definitions.\n\n## What Real Python emphasizes\n\nReal Python first establishes the concepts needed to understand decorators:\n\n1. Functions are first-class objects.\n2. Functions can be passed as arguments.\n3. Functions can be nested.\n4. Functions can be returned from other functions.\n5. A decorator can accept a function, define a wrapper, and return that wrapper.\n\nIts basic pattern is:\n\n```python\ndef decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        # behavior before\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        # behavior after\n        return result\n\n    return wrapper\n```\n\nIt then introduces the shorter form:\n\n```python\n@decorator\ndef function():\n    pass\n```\n\nReal Python also addresses practical issues that the reference documentation does not emphasize, including:\n\n- forwarding arbitrary arguments with `*args` and `**kwargs`\n- preserving function metadata with `functools.wraps`\n- returning values from decorated functions\n- stacking multiple decorators\n- decorating classes\n- creating decorators with their own parameters\n- common applications such as logging, caching, timing, and access control\n\n## Overall assessment\n\nThe sources are complementary rather than contradictory:\n\n- **Use the official documentation** for authoritative syntax, semantics, and evaluation rules.\n- **Use Real Python** for an intuitive explanation, implementation patterns, and practical examples.\n\nIn short, the official documentation explains **what decorators are in Python’s language model**, while Real Python explains **how to understand, write, and apply them in real programs**.\n\n### Sources\n\n- Python documentation glossary — decorator:  \n  https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html\n- Python Language Reference — function definitions:  \n  https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#function-definitions\n- Real Python, *Primer on Python Decorators*:  \n  https://realpython.com/primer-on-python-decorators/', 'output': {'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'response': '## Comparison\n\n| Aspect | Official Python documentation | Real Python |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Authoritative reference for Python’s syntax and semantics | Tutorial designed to build conceptual understanding and practical skill |\n| **Definition** | Treats a decorator as an expression that transforms a function or class during definition | Describes a decorator as a function that wraps another function to modify or extend its behavior |\n| **Core equivalence** | `@dec` is formally equivalent to applying the decorator after the definition: `func = dec(func)` | Demonstrates the same equivalence through runnable examples such as `say_whee = decorator(say_whee)` |\n| **Level of detail** | Concise and technical | Progressive, explanatory, and example-heavy |\n| **Main topics** | Grammar, evaluation, function/class definitions, and language behavior | First-class functions, nested functions, closures, wrappers, arguments, return values, metadata, stacking, and use cases |\n| **Examples** | Minimal examples intended to clarify the specification | Practical examples involving logging, timing, caching, authentication, plugin registration, and more |\n| **Decorator arguments** | Documents the syntax and rules but does not primarily teach implementation patterns | Explains decorators that accept arguments and how to structure them |\n| **Multiple decorators** | Specifies the language behavior | Explains stacking and shows why decorator order affects the result |\n| **Audience** | Developers seeking an authoritative reference | Beginners and intermediate programmers learning how decorators work |\n\n## What the official documentation emphasizes\n\nThe Python documentation focuses on what decorators mean as a language feature. Its glossary explains that decorator syntax is syntactic sugar:\n\n```python\n@dec\ndef func():\n    pass\n```\n\nhas the same effect as:\n\n```python\ndef func():\n    pass\n\nfunc = dec(func)\n```\n\nThe language reference is the better source for precise questions about syntax, evaluation order, and the guarantees made by Python. It also covers decorators applied to class definitions.\n\n## What Real Python emphasizes\n\nReal Python first establishes the concepts needed to understand decorators:\n\n1. Functions are first-class objects.\n2. Functions can be passed as arguments.\n3. Functions can be nested.\n4. Functions can be returned from other functions.\n5. A decorator can accept a function, define a wrapper, and return that wrapper.\n\nIts basic pattern is:\n\n```python\ndef decorator(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        # behavior before\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        # behavior after\n        return result\n\n    return wrapper\n```\n\nIt then introduces the shorter form:\n\n```python\n@decorator\ndef function():\n    pass\n```\n\nReal Python also addresses practical issues that the reference documentation does not emphasize, including:\n\n- forwarding arbitrary arguments with `*args` and `**kwargs`\n- preserving function metadata with `functools.wraps`\n- returning values from decorated functions\n- stacking multiple decorators\n- decorating classes\n- creating decorators with their own parameters\n- common applications such as logging, caching, timing, and access control\n\n## Overall assessment\n\nThe sources are complementary rather than contradictory:\n\n- **Use the official documentation** for authoritative syntax, semantics, and evaluation rules.\n- **Use Real Python** for an intuitive explanation, implementation patterns, and practical examples.\n\nIn short, the official documentation explains **what decorators are in Python’s language model**, while Real Python explains **how to understand, write, and apply them in real programs**.\n\n### Sources\n\n- Python documentation glossary — decorator:  \n  https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html\n- Python Language Reference — function definitions:  \n  https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#function-definitions\n- Real Python, *Primer on Python Decorators*:  \n  https://realpython.com/primer-on-python-decorators/', 'tool_calls': []}, 'error': None, 'thinking': ''}
