18:04:58 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for Summary with this data : {'agent_id': 'Summary', 'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'message': '## Prompt injection attacks: authoritative summary\n\n### What prompt injection is\n\nPrompt injection is an attack in which untrusted input changes an AI system’s behavior by interfering with higher-trust instructions. NIST defines it as exploiting “the concatenation of untrusted input with a prompt constructed by a higher-trust party such as the application designer.”\n\nThe underlying issue is architectural: language models often receive instructions and data in the same natural-language context, so the model may not reliably distinguish “text to analyze” from “instructions to follow.”\n\n### Main forms\n\n- **Direct injection:** Malicious instructions are placed directly in a user’s prompt.\n- **Indirect injection:** Instructions are embedded in webpages, emails, documents, code repositories, search results, or RAG content that an AI system later retrieves and processes.\n- **Obfuscated or hidden injection:** Payloads use encoding, unusual formatting, invisible text, multilingual content, typoglycemia, or other transformations.\n- **Multimodal injection:** Instructions are hidden in images, PDFs, metadata, or other non-text inputs.\n- **Agent and tool attacks:** The injection attempts to make an AI agent call tools, access data, send messages, execute code, or perform other unauthorized actions.\n- **RAG poisoning:** Attacker-controlled documents or vector-store entries are inserted or modified so they influence later model responses.\n\nOWASP notes that injections do not need to be visible or readable to humans; they only need to be interpreted by the model.\n\n### Potential impact\n\nDepending on the application’s permissions, successful attacks can cause:\n\n- Disclosure of system prompts, configuration, or sensitive data\n- Data exfiltration through responses, links, images, or tools\n- Safety-policy bypass or harmful content generation\n- Unauthorized access to APIs, databases, files, or other functions\n- Arbitrary commands or unintended tool calls\n- Manipulated summaries, recommendations, or business decisions\n- Persistent or multi-turn manipulation\n\nThe risk is therefore much greater for an agent with email, filesystem, database, browser, or code-execution access than for a read-only chatbot.\n\n### What authoritative guidance recommends\n\nThere is no universally reliable prompt-only defense. OWASP explicitly states that RAG and fine-tuning do not fully eliminate prompt-injection vulnerabilities. Recommended controls focus on limiting the consequences of a successful injection:\n\n1. **Treat model output and decisions as untrusted.** Do not use the model as the sole enforcement mechanism for authorization or security policy.\n2. **Separate instructions from data.** Clearly identify external content as untrusted and use structured inputs where possible.\n3. **Apply least privilege.** Give the model only the tools, data, and permissions required for its task.\n4. **Validate tool calls outside the model.** Enforce authorization, parameter restrictions, destination limits, and user/session context in deterministic application code.\n5. **Require human approval** for high-impact actions such as sending messages, changing records, transferring funds, or executing code.\n6. **Validate outputs.** Check schemas, grounding, sensitive-data leakage, and compliance with application constraints.\n7. **Sanitize and monitor external content.** Treat webpages, files, emails, retrieved documents, and tool results as attacker-controlled.\n8. **Test continuously.** Include direct, indirect, obfuscated, multimodal, multi-turn, and agent/tool-use attacks in adversarial testing.\n9. **Monitor and contain failures.** Use logging, rate limits, isolation, circuit breakers, and rapid revocation of credentials where appropriate.\n\n### Assessment of the sources\n\n- **NIST** is the strongest source for an official definition and broader AI-risk terminology.\n- **OWASP LLM01:2025** is the most directly relevant industry taxonomy and mitigation guidance.\n- **The OWASP Cheat Sheet** is useful for implementation patterns and testing, but its sample filters should not be treated as complete protection.\n- **NIST AI 600-1** provides broader governance and risk-management guidance rather than a prompt-injection-specific defense.\n\n### Bottom line\n\nPrompt injection is best understood as an **application-security and trust-boundary problem**, not simply a problem of writing better prompts. Robust systems assume that some injections will succeed and ensure that the model cannot use that success to cross authorization boundaries, expose sensitive information, or perform consequential actions without independent controls.\n\n### Sources\n\n- NIST CSRC Glossary — Prompt Injection:  \n  https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/prompt_injection\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project — LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection:  \n  https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\n- OWASP LLM Prompt Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet:  \n  https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1):  \n  https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence\n- NIST AI 600-1 DOI:  \n  https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1', 'output': {'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'response': '## Prompt injection attacks: authoritative summary\n\n### What prompt injection is\n\nPrompt injection is an attack in which untrusted input changes an AI system’s behavior by interfering with higher-trust instructions. NIST defines it as exploiting “the concatenation of untrusted input with a prompt constructed by a higher-trust party such as the application designer.”\n\nThe underlying issue is architectural: language models often receive instructions and data in the same natural-language context, so the model may not reliably distinguish “text to analyze” from “instructions to follow.”\n\n### Main forms\n\n- **Direct injection:** Malicious instructions are placed directly in a user’s prompt.\n- **Indirect injection:** Instructions are embedded in webpages, emails, documents, code repositories, search results, or RAG content that an AI system later retrieves and processes.\n- **Obfuscated or hidden injection:** Payloads use encoding, unusual formatting, invisible text, multilingual content, typoglycemia, or other transformations.\n- **Multimodal injection:** Instructions are hidden in images, PDFs, metadata, or other non-text inputs.\n- **Agent and tool attacks:** The injection attempts to make an AI agent call tools, access data, send messages, execute code, or perform other unauthorized actions.\n- **RAG poisoning:** Attacker-controlled documents or vector-store entries are inserted or modified so they influence later model responses.\n\nOWASP notes that injections do not need to be visible or readable to humans; they only need to be interpreted by the model.\n\n### Potential impact\n\nDepending on the application’s permissions, successful attacks can cause:\n\n- Disclosure of system prompts, configuration, or sensitive data\n- Data exfiltration through responses, links, images, or tools\n- Safety-policy bypass or harmful content generation\n- Unauthorized access to APIs, databases, files, or other functions\n- Arbitrary commands or unintended tool calls\n- Manipulated summaries, recommendations, or business decisions\n- Persistent or multi-turn manipulation\n\nThe risk is therefore much greater for an agent with email, filesystem, database, browser, or code-execution access than for a read-only chatbot.\n\n### What authoritative guidance recommends\n\nThere is no universally reliable prompt-only defense. OWASP explicitly states that RAG and fine-tuning do not fully eliminate prompt-injection vulnerabilities. Recommended controls focus on limiting the consequences of a successful injection:\n\n1. **Treat model output and decisions as untrusted.** Do not use the model as the sole enforcement mechanism for authorization or security policy.\n2. **Separate instructions from data.** Clearly identify external content as untrusted and use structured inputs where possible.\n3. **Apply least privilege.** Give the model only the tools, data, and permissions required for its task.\n4. **Validate tool calls outside the model.** Enforce authorization, parameter restrictions, destination limits, and user/session context in deterministic application code.\n5. **Require human approval** for high-impact actions such as sending messages, changing records, transferring funds, or executing code.\n6. **Validate outputs.** Check schemas, grounding, sensitive-data leakage, and compliance with application constraints.\n7. **Sanitize and monitor external content.** Treat webpages, files, emails, retrieved documents, and tool results as attacker-controlled.\n8. **Test continuously.** Include direct, indirect, obfuscated, multimodal, multi-turn, and agent/tool-use attacks in adversarial testing.\n9. **Monitor and contain failures.** Use logging, rate limits, isolation, circuit breakers, and rapid revocation of credentials where appropriate.\n\n### Assessment of the sources\n\n- **NIST** is the strongest source for an official definition and broader AI-risk terminology.\n- **OWASP LLM01:2025** is the most directly relevant industry taxonomy and mitigation guidance.\n- **The OWASP Cheat Sheet** is useful for implementation patterns and testing, but its sample filters should not be treated as complete protection.\n- **NIST AI 600-1** provides broader governance and risk-management guidance rather than a prompt-injection-specific defense.\n\n### Bottom line\n\nPrompt injection is best understood as an **application-security and trust-boundary problem**, not simply a problem of writing better prompts. Robust systems assume that some injections will succeed and ensure that the model cannot use that success to cross authorization boundaries, expose sensitive information, or perform consequential actions without independent controls.\n\n### Sources\n\n- NIST CSRC Glossary — Prompt Injection:  \n  https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/prompt_injection\n- OWASP GenAI Security Project — LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection:  \n  https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\n- OWASP LLM Prompt Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet:  \n  https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework: Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1):  \n  https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence\n- NIST AI 600-1 DOI:  \n  https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1', 'tool_calls': []}, 'error': None, 'thinking': ''}
