18:12:34 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for Summary with this data : {'agent_id': 'Summary', 'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'message': 'PostgreSQL 18 is a substantial evolution of PostgreSQL 17, with its biggest gains in I/O performance, upgrades, indexing, developer features, and authentication.\n\n| Area | PostgreSQL 17 | PostgreSQL 18 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Release date** | September 26, 2024 | September 25, 2025 |\n| **I/O performance** | Improved streaming reads, WAL throughput, and vacuum efficiency | Adds a new asynchronous I/O subsystem for sequential scans, bitmap scans, and vacuum; some workloads may see up to 3× faster storage reads |\n| **Query planning** | Improvements to CTEs, partition pruning, `IS NULL`, correlated subqueries, and multi-value index searches | Adds B-tree **skip scans**, better `OR`-to-index transformations, improved hash and merge joins, partition planning, and self-join elimination |\n| **Major upgrades** | `pg_upgrade` preserves logical replication slots and subscription state | `pg_upgrade` can preserve most optimizer statistics, reducing poor query performance immediately after upgrading; also adds parallel checks and `--swap` |\n| **Generated columns** | Generated columns were stored | Virtual generated columns are supported and are now the default; stored generated columns can be logically replicated |\n| **DML** | `MERGE` gained `RETURNING` and `WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE` | `RETURNING` can explicitly expose both `OLD` and `NEW` row values for `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `MERGE` |\n| **JSON and data types** | Major SQL/JSON additions, including `JSON_TABLE()`, `JSON_VALUE()`, and `JSON_QUERY()` | Adds `uuidv7()`, array sorting and reversal functions, improved Unicode handling, and more flexible JSONB-null casts |\n| **Constraints** | Exclusion constraints on partitioned tables | Temporal constraints using `WITHOUT OVERLAPS` and `PERIOD`; also supports `NOT ENFORCED` constraints |\n| **Authentication** | Direct TLS negotiation and `transaction_timeout` | OAuth authentication, TLS 1.3 cipher configuration, SCRAM passthrough, and deprecation of MD5 password authentication |\n| **Replication and backup** | Incremental `pg_basebackup`, WAL summarization, logical-replication failover, and `pg_createsubscriber` | Parallel logical-replication streaming by default, automatic invalidation of idle replication slots, improved conflict reporting, and more `pg_createsubscriber` options |\n| **Monitoring** | New `pg_stat_checkpointer`, `pg_wait_events`, and richer `EXPLAIN` output | More proactive vacuum freezing, automatic `BUFFERS` in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`, index-lookup counts, and more per-backend I/O and WAL statistics |\n| **Defaults** | Existing checksum settings were generally retained | New clusters created with `initdb` enable data checksums by default |\n\n### The most important practical differences\n\n1. **PostgreSQL 18 is generally better for I/O-heavy workloads.** Its asynchronous I/O system can issue multiple reads concurrently, improving sequential scans, bitmap scans, and vacuum.\n\n2. **Upgrades should have a shorter performance recovery period.** PostgreSQL 18 preserves optimizer statistics during `pg_upgrade`, so the server does not have to rebuild all statistics before query plans return to normal.\n\n3. **Multicolumn indexes can be useful in more cases.** B-tree skip scans allow PostgreSQL to use an index even when a query does not constrain the index’s first column—for example, under suitable conditions with an index on `(a, b)` and a filter primarily on `b`.\n\n4. **PostgreSQL 18 adds notable application-development features.** These include virtual generated columns, timestamp-ordered UUIDs through `uuidv7()`, temporal constraints, and access to both old and new row values in `RETURNING`.\n\n5. **Authentication is moving away from MD5.** PostgreSQL 18 supports OAuth and deprecates MD5 password authentication. Existing MD5 configurations should be migrated to SCRAM.\n\n6. **PostgreSQL 17’s signature features remain important.** PostgreSQL 17 introduced major SQL/JSON functionality, incremental physical backups, logical-replication failover support, and significant vacuum improvements. PostgreSQL 18 builds on rather than replaces these capabilities.\n\n### Upgrade considerations\n\nPostgreSQL 17 to 18 is a **major-version upgrade**, not a minor update. You must use `pg_upgrade`, dump/restore, or logical replication. Before upgrading, pay particular attention to:\n\n- checksum compatibility between old and new clusters;\n- MD5 authentication deprecation;\n- changed `COPY` behavior;\n- collation and full-text-search indexes;\n- generated-column behavior;\n- compatibility changes listed in the PostgreSQL 18 migration notes.\n\n**Sources:**\n\n- [PostgreSQL 18 release notes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/release-18.html)\n- [PostgreSQL 17 release notes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/release/17.0/)\n- [PostgreSQL 18 official announcement](https://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-18-released-3142/)\n- [PostgreSQL upgrade documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/upgrading.html)', 'output': {'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'response': 'PostgreSQL 18 is a substantial evolution of PostgreSQL 17, with its biggest gains in I/O performance, upgrades, indexing, developer features, and authentication.\n\n| Area | PostgreSQL 17 | PostgreSQL 18 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Release date** | September 26, 2024 | September 25, 2025 |\n| **I/O performance** | Improved streaming reads, WAL throughput, and vacuum efficiency | Adds a new asynchronous I/O subsystem for sequential scans, bitmap scans, and vacuum; some workloads may see up to 3× faster storage reads |\n| **Query planning** | Improvements to CTEs, partition pruning, `IS NULL`, correlated subqueries, and multi-value index searches | Adds B-tree **skip scans**, better `OR`-to-index transformations, improved hash and merge joins, partition planning, and self-join elimination |\n| **Major upgrades** | `pg_upgrade` preserves logical replication slots and subscription state | `pg_upgrade` can preserve most optimizer statistics, reducing poor query performance immediately after upgrading; also adds parallel checks and `--swap` |\n| **Generated columns** | Generated columns were stored | Virtual generated columns are supported and are now the default; stored generated columns can be logically replicated |\n| **DML** | `MERGE` gained `RETURNING` and `WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE` | `RETURNING` can explicitly expose both `OLD` and `NEW` row values for `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `MERGE` |\n| **JSON and data types** | Major SQL/JSON additions, including `JSON_TABLE()`, `JSON_VALUE()`, and `JSON_QUERY()` | Adds `uuidv7()`, array sorting and reversal functions, improved Unicode handling, and more flexible JSONB-null casts |\n| **Constraints** | Exclusion constraints on partitioned tables | Temporal constraints using `WITHOUT OVERLAPS` and `PERIOD`; also supports `NOT ENFORCED` constraints |\n| **Authentication** | Direct TLS negotiation and `transaction_timeout` | OAuth authentication, TLS 1.3 cipher configuration, SCRAM passthrough, and deprecation of MD5 password authentication |\n| **Replication and backup** | Incremental `pg_basebackup`, WAL summarization, logical-replication failover, and `pg_createsubscriber` | Parallel logical-replication streaming by default, automatic invalidation of idle replication slots, improved conflict reporting, and more `pg_createsubscriber` options |\n| **Monitoring** | New `pg_stat_checkpointer`, `pg_wait_events`, and richer `EXPLAIN` output | More proactive vacuum freezing, automatic `BUFFERS` in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`, index-lookup counts, and more per-backend I/O and WAL statistics |\n| **Defaults** | Existing checksum settings were generally retained | New clusters created with `initdb` enable data checksums by default |\n\n### The most important practical differences\n\n1. **PostgreSQL 18 is generally better for I/O-heavy workloads.** Its asynchronous I/O system can issue multiple reads concurrently, improving sequential scans, bitmap scans, and vacuum.\n\n2. **Upgrades should have a shorter performance recovery period.** PostgreSQL 18 preserves optimizer statistics during `pg_upgrade`, so the server does not have to rebuild all statistics before query plans return to normal.\n\n3. **Multicolumn indexes can be useful in more cases.** B-tree skip scans allow PostgreSQL to use an index even when a query does not constrain the index’s first column—for example, under suitable conditions with an index on `(a, b)` and a filter primarily on `b`.\n\n4. **PostgreSQL 18 adds notable application-development features.** These include virtual generated columns, timestamp-ordered UUIDs through `uuidv7()`, temporal constraints, and access to both old and new row values in `RETURNING`.\n\n5. **Authentication is moving away from MD5.** PostgreSQL 18 supports OAuth and deprecates MD5 password authentication. Existing MD5 configurations should be migrated to SCRAM.\n\n6. **PostgreSQL 17’s signature features remain important.** PostgreSQL 17 introduced major SQL/JSON functionality, incremental physical backups, logical-replication failover support, and significant vacuum improvements. PostgreSQL 18 builds on rather than replaces these capabilities.\n\n### Upgrade considerations\n\nPostgreSQL 17 to 18 is a **major-version upgrade**, not a minor update. You must use `pg_upgrade`, dump/restore, or logical replication. Before upgrading, pay particular attention to:\n\n- checksum compatibility between old and new clusters;\n- MD5 authentication deprecation;\n- changed `COPY` behavior;\n- collation and full-text-search indexes;\n- generated-column behavior;\n- compatibility changes listed in the PostgreSQL 18 migration notes.\n\n**Sources:**\n\n- [PostgreSQL 18 release notes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/release-18.html)\n- [PostgreSQL 17 release notes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/release/17.0/)\n- [PostgreSQL 18 official announcement](https://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-18-released-3142/)\n- [PostgreSQL upgrade documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/upgrading.html)', 'tool_calls': []}, 'error': None, 'thinking': ''}
