18:17:48 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for Summary with this data : {'agent_id': 'Summary', 'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'message': 'Python 3.14 documents `asyncio.TaskGroup` as an **asynchronous context manager for structured concurrency**. It combines task creation with reliable waiting and exception handling for related tasks.\n\nKey points:\n\n- Create tasks with `tg.create_task(coro)`.\n- Exiting the `async with` block waits for every task in the group.\n- Tasks can be added while the group is active, including by tasks already in the group. Once shutdown begins, no new tasks may be added.\n- If a task raises anything other than `asyncio.CancelledError`, the remaining tasks are cancelled and awaited.\n- Exceptions from failed tasks are combined into an `ExceptionGroup` or `BaseExceptionGroup`.\n- `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit` are re-raised after the other tasks finish.\n- An exception raised by the body of the `async with` block is handled similarly and included in the exception group, except for `CancelledError`.\n- Nested groups and simultaneous internal/external cancellation are handled carefully. The documentation notes that Python 3.13 improved cancellation handling and preservation of cancellation counts.\n- In Python 3.14, `TaskGroup.create_task()` passes all keyword arguments through to the event loop’s `create_task()`. If the group is inactive, the coroutine is closed and `RuntimeError` is raised.\n- Compared with `asyncio.gather()`, `TaskGroup` provides stronger safety guarantees: if a task or nested subtask fails, sibling tasks are cancelled.\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n        task1 = tg.create_task(some_coro(...))\n        task2 = tg.create_task(another_coro(...))\n\n    # Both tasks have completed here\n    print(task1.result(), task2.result())\n```\n\nThe documentation says `TaskGroup` was added in Python 3.11; the Python 3.14 change concerns forwarding all keyword arguments in `create_task()`.\n\nSource: [Python 3.14 — Coroutines and tasks](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/asyncio-task.html)', 'output': {'role': 'You are the research summary specialist.\nYou have no tools. Read the original user request and the complete web-search context from previous age. \nSynthesize a clear, accurate answer, distinguish sourced facts from uncertainty, and include the relevant source URLs.\nAnswer the user directly rather than describing your internal process.', 'response': 'Python 3.14 documents `asyncio.TaskGroup` as an **asynchronous context manager for structured concurrency**. It combines task creation with reliable waiting and exception handling for related tasks.\n\nKey points:\n\n- Create tasks with `tg.create_task(coro)`.\n- Exiting the `async with` block waits for every task in the group.\n- Tasks can be added while the group is active, including by tasks already in the group. Once shutdown begins, no new tasks may be added.\n- If a task raises anything other than `asyncio.CancelledError`, the remaining tasks are cancelled and awaited.\n- Exceptions from failed tasks are combined into an `ExceptionGroup` or `BaseExceptionGroup`.\n- `KeyboardInterrupt` and `SystemExit` are re-raised after the other tasks finish.\n- An exception raised by the body of the `async with` block is handled similarly and included in the exception group, except for `CancelledError`.\n- Nested groups and simultaneous internal/external cancellation are handled carefully. The documentation notes that Python 3.13 improved cancellation handling and preservation of cancellation counts.\n- In Python 3.14, `TaskGroup.create_task()` passes all keyword arguments through to the event loop’s `create_task()`. If the group is inactive, the coroutine is closed and `RuntimeError` is raised.\n- Compared with `asyncio.gather()`, `TaskGroup` provides stronger safety guarantees: if a task or nested subtask fails, sibling tasks are cancelled.\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n        task1 = tg.create_task(some_coro(...))\n        task2 = tg.create_task(another_coro(...))\n\n    # Both tasks have completed here\n    print(task1.result(), task2.result())\n```\n\nThe documentation says `TaskGroup` was added in Python 3.11; the Python 3.14 change concerns forwarding all keyword arguments in `create_task()`.\n\nSource: [Python 3.14 — Coroutines and tasks](https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/asyncio-task.html)', 'tool_calls': []}, 'error': None, 'thinking': ''}
