18:18:26 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for WebSearch with this data : {'agent_id': 'WebSearch', 'role': 'You are the web and academic research specialist.\nUse web_search for current facts, official documentation, news, and broad public-web research.\nUse fetch_web_page after web_search when exact page content is needed rather than relying on a snippet.\nSearch multiple times when different queries or source types are needed.\nPrefer primary and authoritative sources, preserve stable URLs, and do not invent facts.\nOnce enough evidence has been collected, provide a concise research result with the sources for the Summary agent.', 'message': 'According to the **Wikipedia Apollo 11 page**, the lunar module *Eagle* landed on the Moon on **July 20, 1969, at 20:17:40 UTC** (8:17:40 p.m. UTC).\n\nSource: [Apollo 11 – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11)', 'output': {'role': 'You are the web and academic research specialist.\nUse web_search for current facts, official documentation, news, and broad public-web research.\nUse fetch_web_page after web_search when exact page content is needed rather than relying on a snippet.\nSearch multiple times when different queries or source types are needed.\nPrefer primary and authoritative sources, preserve stable URLs, and do not invent facts.\nOnce enough evidence has been collected, provide a concise research result with the sources for the Summary agent.', 'response': 'According to the **Wikipedia Apollo 11 page**, the lunar module *Eagle* landed on the Moon on **July 20, 1969, at 20:17:40 UTC** (8:17:40 p.m. UTC).\n\nSource: [Apollo 11 – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11)', 'tool_calls': [{'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_XwQRCenUYnjrLEBvM6OnhosJ', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11 Apollo 11 landing date time 20 July 1969 20:17:40 UTC'}, 'result': "No web results found for 'site:en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11 Apollo 11 landing date time 20 July 1969 20:17:40 UTC'.", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 2, 'id': 'call_TXApFUcMimcMPv7SEtwrxHcH', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'Wikipedia Apollo 11 landing 20:17:40 UTC July 20 1969'}, 'result': '[1] Apollo 11 - Wikipedia\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11\nSnippet: Eagle landed at 20:17:40 UTC on Sunday July 20 with 216 pounds (98 kg) of usable fuel remaining. Information available to the crew and mission controllers during the landing showed the LM had enough fuel for another 25 seconds of powered flight before an abort without touchdown would have become unsafe, [10][129] but post-mission analysis ...\n\n[2] Tranquility Base - Wikipedia\nURL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquility_Base\nSnippet: Tranquility Base (Latin: Statio Tranquillitatis) is the site on the Moon where, in July 1969, American NASA astronauts landed and walked on a celestial body other than Earth for the first time. On July 20, 1969, Apollo 11 crewmembers Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed their Apollo Lunar Module Eagle at approximately 20:17:40 UTC.\n\n[3] 20 July 1969, 20:17:39 UTC, T + 102:45:39.9 - This Day in Aviation\nURL: https://www.thisdayinaviation.com/20-july-1969-201740-utc/\nSnippet: The Apollo 11 Lunar Module Eagle, with Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin aboard, shortly after separation from the Command and Service Module, in orbit around the Moon, 20 July 1969. (Michael Collins/National Aeronautics and Space Administration AS11-44-6598) 102:45:25 Aldrin: Four forward. Four forward. Drifting to the right a little.\n\n[4] Apollo 11 Timeline - National Air and Space Museum\nURL: https://airandspace.si.edu/explore/stories/apollo-missions/apollo-11-moon-landing/apollo-11-timeline\nSnippet: The Apollo 11 Lunar Module Eagle, in a landing configuration, was photographed in lunar orbit from the Command and Service Module Columbia. July 20, 1969 17:44 UTC 1:44 pm ET The lunar module with Armstrong and Aldrin aboard was undocked from the command module. At 101:36 GET, the descent to the surface of the Moon began.\n\n[5] Apollo 11 - Wikiwand\nURL: https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Appolo_11\nSnippet: Apollo 11 was the first spaceflight to land humans on the Moon, conducted by NASA from July 16 to 24, 1969. Commander Neil Armstrong and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin "Buzz" Aldrin landed the Lunar Module Eagle on July 20 at 20:17 UTC, and Armstrong became the first person to step onto the surface about six hours later, at 02:56 UTC on July 21.', 'success': True}, {'iteration': 3, 'id': 'call_Ge5GUnlu9Wxjd1GG0axGjvwT', 'name': 'fetch_web_page', 'args': {'url': 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11', 'max_chars': 5000}, 'result': 'URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11\nTitle: Apollo 11 - Wikipedia\nContent:\nApollo 11 - Wikipedia Jump to content Main menu Main menu move to sidebar hide Navigation Main page Contents Current events Random article About Wikipedia Contact us Contribute Help Learn to edit Community portal Recent changes Upload file Special pages Search Search Appearance Donate Create account Log in Personal tools Donate Create account Log in Contents move to sidebar hide (Top) 1 Background 2 Personnel Toggle Personnel subsection 2.1 Prime crew 2.2 Backup crew 2.3 Support crew 2.4 Capsule communicators 2.5 Flight directors 2.6 Other key personnel 3 Preparations Toggle Preparations subsection 3.1 Insignia 3.2 Call signs 3.3 Mementos 3.4 Site selection 3.5 First-step decision 3.6 Pre-launch 4 Mission Toggle Mission subsection 4.1 Launch and flight to lunar orbit 4.2 Lunar descent 4.3 Landing 4.4 Lunar surface operations 4.5 Lunar ascent 4.6 Columbia in lunar orbit 4.7 Return 4.8 Splashdown and quarantine 5 Celebrations Toggle Celebrations subsection 5.1 World tour 6 Legacy Toggle Legacy subsection 6.1 Cultural significance 6.2 Spacecraft 6.3 Moon rocks 6.4 Experiment results 6.5 Moonwalk camera 6.6 Lunar Module Eagle memorabilia 6.7 Anniversary events 6.7.1 40th anniversary 6.7.2 50th anniversary 7 Films and documentaries 8 See also 9 References Toggle References subsection 9.1 Notes 9.2 Citations 9.3 Sources 10 External links Toggle External links subsection 10.1 Multimedia Toggle the table of contents Apollo 11 104 languages Afrikaans አማርኛ Aragonés العربية অসমীয়া Asturianu Azərbaycanca تۆرکجه Basa Bali Беларуская (тарашкевіца) Беларуская Български বাংলা Brezhoneg Bosanski Català Cebuano کوردی Čeština Чӑвашла Cymraeg Dansk Deutsch Ελληνικά Esperanto Español Eesti Euskara فارسی Suomi Français Frysk Gaeilge Galego ગુજરાતી Gaelg Hausa עברית हिन्दी Hrvatski Magyar Հայերեն Bahasa Indonesia Ido Íslenska Italiano 日本語 Jawa ქართული Қазақша ಕನ್ನಡ 한국어 کٲشُر Kurdî Latina Lombard Lietuvių Latviešu Malagasy Minangkabau Македонски മലയാളം मराठी Bahasa Melayu မြန်မာဘာသာ Nederlands Norsk nynorsk Norsk bokmål Occitan ଓଡ଼ିଆ Polski Piemontèis پنجابی پښتو Português Română Русский Саха тыла Srpskohrvatski / српскохрватски සිංහල Simple English Slovenčina Slovenščina Anarâškielâ Shqip Српски / srpski Seeltersk Svenska Kiswahili தமிழ் ไทย Tagalog Türkçe Xitsonga Українська اردو Oʻzbekcha / ўзбекча Vèneto Tiếng Việt Winaray 吴语 ייִדיש 粵語 中文 Edit links Article Talk English Read View source View history Tools Tools move to sidebar hide Actions Read View source View history General What links here Related changes Upload file Permanent link Page information Cite this page Get shortened URL Switch to legacy parser Print/export Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Wikimedia Commons Wikiquote Wikidata item Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia First crewed Moon landing (1969) "First Moon landing" and "The Moon landing" redirect here. For other crewed or uncrewed Moon landings, see Moon landing . This article is about the 1969 crewed lunar mission. For other uses, see Apollo 11 (disambiguation) . Apollo 11 Buzz Aldrin on the Moon in a photograph taken by Neil Armstrong , who can be seen in the visor reflection along with Earth, the Lunar Module Eag