18:21:34 [INFO   ] arco.core.evaluator: Evaluating ground truth data for WebSearch with this data : {'agent_id': 'WebSearch', 'role': 'You are the web and academic research specialist.\nUse web_search for current facts, official documentation, news, and broad public-web research.\nUse fetch_web_page after web_search when exact page content is needed rather than relying on a snippet.\nSearch multiple times when different queries or source types are needed.\nPrefer primary and authoritative sources, preserve stable URLs, and do not invent facts.\nOnce enough evidence has been collected, provide a concise research result with the sources for the Summary agent.', 'message': '## Summary\n\nPrompt injection is an attack against an LLM application in which untrusted text—whether supplied directly by a user or retrieved from external content—causes the model to disregard its intended instructions or produce unauthorized behavior. The central security problem is that LLMs commonly process instructions and data in the same natural-language context, making trust boundaries difficult to enforce.\n\n### Main attack types\n\n- **Direct prompt injection:** The attacker places malicious instructions directly in the user prompt.\n- **Indirect or remote prompt injection:** Malicious instructions are embedded in webpages, emails, documents, code comments, images, or retrieved RAG content that the model later processes.\n- **Obfuscated or multimodal injection:** The payload is hidden through encoding, unusual formatting, invisible text, multilingual content, images, or other modalities.\n- **Agent and tool attacks:** The injection attempts to make an LLM agent call tools, access data, send messages, execute code, or take other actions beyond the user’s authorization.\n- **RAG poisoning:** Attacker-controlled documents or vector-store content influence the model when retrieved as supposedly relevant context.\n\n### Potential consequences\n\nAuthoritative guidance identifies risks including:\n\n- System-prompt or configuration disclosure\n- Sensitive-data exfiltration\n- Safety-policy bypass and harmful content generation\n- Unauthorized access to functions, APIs, or connected systems\n- Arbitrary commands or unintended tool calls\n- Manipulated business decisions or misleading summaries\n- Cross-modal attacks involving images or documents\n\nThe severity depends heavily on the application’s permissions. A prompt injection in a read-only chatbot is generally less consequential than one in an agent with email, database, filesystem, or code-execution access.\n\n### Recommended defenses\n\nNo single prompt-level defense is considered foolproof. The strongest guidance emphasizes reducing impact through application architecture:\n\n1. **Treat the model as untrusted.** Do not rely on the model to enforce authorization or security boundaries.\n2. **Separate instructions from untrusted data.** Clearly label external content and use structured inputs where possible.\n3. **Apply least privilege.** Give the model only the minimum tool and data access required.\n4. **Validate tool calls in deterministic code.** Enforce authorization, parameter constraints, destination restrictions, and user/session context outside the model.\n5. **Require human approval for high-risk actions.**\n6. **Validate outputs.** Check format, grounding, sensitive-data leakage, and whether responses meet application constraints.\n7. **Sanitize and monitor external content.** Treat webpages, files, emails, retrieved documents, and tool outputs as untrusted.\n8. **Use adversarial testing and continuous monitoring.** Test direct, indirect, obfuscated, multimodal, and multi-turn attacks.\n9. **Do not assume RAG or fine-tuning solves the problem.** OWASP explicitly notes that these techniques do not fully mitigate prompt injection.\n\n## Most authoritative sources\n\n1. **NIST CSRC Glossary — Prompt Injection**  \n   Official U.S. government definition: an attack exploiting the concatenation of untrusted input with a prompt constructed by a higher-trust party, such as the application designer.  \n   https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/prompt_injection\n\n2. **OWASP GenAI Security Project — LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection**  \n   The most directly relevant industry risk classification. It covers direct and indirect injection, impacts, multimodal attacks, examples, and mitigation strategies such as least privilege, external-content separation, output validation, human approval, and adversarial testing.  \n   https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\n\n3. **OWASP Cheat Sheet — LLM Prompt Injection Prevention**  \n   Practical engineering guidance covering attack patterns, RAG poisoning, agent-specific attacks, system-prompt extraction, data exfiltration, obfuscation, output monitoring, tool restrictions, and testing. It is useful for implementation, but its example filters should not be treated as complete protection.  \n   https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\n\n4. **NIST AI RMF: Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1)**  \n   A broader risk-management framework rather than a prompt-injection-specific guide. It provides governance, measurement, and lifecycle practices for managing generative-AI risks, including security and evaluation concerns.  \n   https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence  \n   DOI: https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1\n\n## Bottom line\n\nPrompt injection is best understood as an **application security and trust-boundary problem**, not merely a bad-prompt problem. Prompt wording, filters, and model training can reduce risk, but robust systems must assume that injections will sometimes succeed and ensure that success cannot directly expose sensitive data or trigger unauthorized actions. The most reliable controls are external authorization, least-privilege tool access, deterministic validation, isolation of untrusted content, monitoring, and human approval for consequential operations.', 'output': {'role': 'You are the web and academic research specialist.\nUse web_search for current facts, official documentation, news, and broad public-web research.\nUse fetch_web_page after web_search when exact page content is needed rather than relying on a snippet.\nSearch multiple times when different queries or source types are needed.\nPrefer primary and authoritative sources, preserve stable URLs, and do not invent facts.\nOnce enough evidence has been collected, provide a concise research result with the sources for the Summary agent.', 'response': '## Summary\n\nPrompt injection is an attack against an LLM application in which untrusted text—whether supplied directly by a user or retrieved from external content—causes the model to disregard its intended instructions or produce unauthorized behavior. The central security problem is that LLMs commonly process instructions and data in the same natural-language context, making trust boundaries difficult to enforce.\n\n### Main attack types\n\n- **Direct prompt injection:** The attacker places malicious instructions directly in the user prompt.\n- **Indirect or remote prompt injection:** Malicious instructions are embedded in webpages, emails, documents, code comments, images, or retrieved RAG content that the model later processes.\n- **Obfuscated or multimodal injection:** The payload is hidden through encoding, unusual formatting, invisible text, multilingual content, images, or other modalities.\n- **Agent and tool attacks:** The injection attempts to make an LLM agent call tools, access data, send messages, execute code, or take other actions beyond the user’s authorization.\n- **RAG poisoning:** Attacker-controlled documents or vector-store content influence the model when retrieved as supposedly relevant context.\n\n### Potential consequences\n\nAuthoritative guidance identifies risks including:\n\n- System-prompt or configuration disclosure\n- Sensitive-data exfiltration\n- Safety-policy bypass and harmful content generation\n- Unauthorized access to functions, APIs, or connected systems\n- Arbitrary commands or unintended tool calls\n- Manipulated business decisions or misleading summaries\n- Cross-modal attacks involving images or documents\n\nThe severity depends heavily on the application’s permissions. A prompt injection in a read-only chatbot is generally less consequential than one in an agent with email, database, filesystem, or code-execution access.\n\n### Recommended defenses\n\nNo single prompt-level defense is considered foolproof. The strongest guidance emphasizes reducing impact through application architecture:\n\n1. **Treat the model as untrusted.** Do not rely on the model to enforce authorization or security boundaries.\n2. **Separate instructions from untrusted data.** Clearly label external content and use structured inputs where possible.\n3. **Apply least privilege.** Give the model only the minimum tool and data access required.\n4. **Validate tool calls in deterministic code.** Enforce authorization, parameter constraints, destination restrictions, and user/session context outside the model.\n5. **Require human approval for high-risk actions.**\n6. **Validate outputs.** Check format, grounding, sensitive-data leakage, and whether responses meet application constraints.\n7. **Sanitize and monitor external content.** Treat webpages, files, emails, retrieved documents, and tool outputs as untrusted.\n8. **Use adversarial testing and continuous monitoring.** Test direct, indirect, obfuscated, multimodal, and multi-turn attacks.\n9. **Do not assume RAG or fine-tuning solves the problem.** OWASP explicitly notes that these techniques do not fully mitigate prompt injection.\n\n## Most authoritative sources\n\n1. **NIST CSRC Glossary — Prompt Injection**  \n   Official U.S. government definition: an attack exploiting the concatenation of untrusted input with a prompt constructed by a higher-trust party, such as the application designer.  \n   https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/prompt_injection\n\n2. **OWASP GenAI Security Project — LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection**  \n   The most directly relevant industry risk classification. It covers direct and indirect injection, impacts, multimodal attacks, examples, and mitigation strategies such as least privilege, external-content separation, output validation, human approval, and adversarial testing.  \n   https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\n\n3. **OWASP Cheat Sheet — LLM Prompt Injection Prevention**  \n   Practical engineering guidance covering attack patterns, RAG poisoning, agent-specific attacks, system-prompt extraction, data exfiltration, obfuscation, output monitoring, tool restrictions, and testing. It is useful for implementation, but its example filters should not be treated as complete protection.  \n   https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\n\n4. **NIST AI RMF: Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1)**  \n   A broader risk-management framework rather than a prompt-injection-specific guide. It provides governance, measurement, and lifecycle practices for managing generative-AI risks, including security and evaluation concerns.  \n   https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence  \n   DOI: https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1\n\n## Bottom line\n\nPrompt injection is best understood as an **application security and trust-boundary problem**, not merely a bad-prompt problem. Prompt wording, filters, and model training can reduce risk, but robust systems must assume that injections will sometimes succeed and ensure that success cannot directly expose sensitive data or trigger unauthorized actions. The most reliable controls are external authorization, least-privilege tool access, deterministic validation, isolation of untrusted content, monitoring, and human approval for consequential operations.', 'tool_calls': [{'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_WHrnd6Ps7wzV1LsJHg0U0LU3', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:owasp.org prompt injection prevention cheat sheet LLM01 official'}, 'result': "[1] LLM Prompt Injection Prevention - OWASP Cheat Sheet Series\nURL: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\nSnippet: LLM Prompt Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet Introduction ¶ Prompt injection is a vulnerability in Large Language Model (LLM) applications that allows attackers to manipulate the model's behavior by injecting malicious input that changes its intended output.\n\n[2] LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection - OWASP Gen AI Security Project\nURL: https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\nSnippet: A Prompt Injection Vulnerability occurs when user prompts alter the LLM's behavior or output in unintended ways. These inputs can affect the model even if they are imperceptible to humans, therefore prompt injections do not need to be human-visible/readable, as long as the content is parsed by the model. Prompt Injection vulnerabilities exist in how […]\n\n[3] LLM01: Prompt Injection - OWASP Gen AI Security Project\nURL: https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk2023-24/llm01-24-prompt-injection/\nSnippet: Discover LLM01: Prompt Injection—how attackers manipulate prompts to alter model behavior, leak data, or bypass intended restrictions in generative AI systems.\n\n[4] Prompt Injection | OWASP Foundation\nURL: https://community.owasp.org/attacks/PromptInjection\nSnippet: Prompt Injection is a novel security vulnerability that targets Large Language Models (LLMs) like ChatGPT, Bard, and others. It manipulates the model's behavior by crafting malicious or misleading prompts—often bypassing safety filters and executing unintended instructions.\n\n[5] LLM01:2023 - Prompt Injections - OWASP Foundation\nURL: https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/Archive/0_1_vulns/Prompt_Injection\nSnippet: LLM01:2023 - Prompt Injections Description: Prompt injections involve bypassing filters or manipulating the LLM using carefully crafted prompts that make the model ignore previous instructions or perform unintended actions. These vulnerabilities can lead to unintended consequences, including data leakage, unauthorized access, or other security breaches. Common Prompt Injection Vulnerabilities ...", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_0wM4rx0r7TFzi87ZzKfQ6hUo', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:nist.gov prompt injection generative AI official'}, 'result': "[1] prompt injection - Glossary | CSRC\nURL: https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/prompt_injection\nSnippet: prompt injection Definitions: An attack which exploits the concatenation of untrusted input with a prompt constructed by a higher-trust party such as the application designer. Sources: NIST AI 100-2e2025\n\n[2] PDF Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative ... - NIST\nURL: https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\nSnippet: 1. Introduction This document is a cross-sectoral profile of and companion resource for the AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0) for Generative AI,1 pursuant to President Biden's Executive Order (EO) 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence.2 The AI RMF was released in January 2023, and is intended for voluntary use and to improve the ability of organizations to ...\n\n[3] Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative ...\nURL: https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence\nSnippet: Abstract This document is a cross-sectoral profile of and companion resource for the AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0) for Generative AI, pursuant to President Biden's Executive Order (EO) 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence. The AI RMF was released in January 2023, and is intended for voluntary use and to improve the ability of organizations to incorporate ...\n\n[4] AI Risk Management Framework | NIST\nURL: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\nSnippet: On July 26, 2024, NIST released NIST-AI-600-1, Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile . The profile can help organizations identify unique risks posed by generative AI and proposes actions for generative AI risk management that best aligns with their goals and priorities.\n\n[5] Nvd-cve-2025-46059\nURL: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2025-46059\nSnippet: Description langchain-ai v0.3.51 was discovered to contain an indirect prompt injection vulnerability in the GmailToolkit component. This vulnerability allows attackers to execute arbitrary code and compromise the application via a crafted email message.", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_FefXwupoac9MvevJ2EBdS79q', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:learn.microsoft.com prompt injection attacks generative AI official'}, 'result': "No web results found for 'site:learn.microsoft.com prompt injection attacks generative AI official'.", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_p5d76zrNEv5Q2D5I5j2rqQeD', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:genai.owasp.org prompt injection LLM01 official'}, 'result': "No web results found for 'site:genai.owasp.org prompt injection LLM01 official'.", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_YHOzltatlo1N68r9TZXMwZVD', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'prompt injection attacks academic paper indirect prompt injection arxiv'}, 'result': "[1] Will the User Ever Know? Covert Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks on ...\nURL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.30362\nSnippet: As LLM agents take real-world actions through tools, indirect prompt injection (IPI) has emerged as a serious threat. The standard metric, Attack Success Rate (ASR), counts whether an injection succeeds but ignores what the user notices in the agent's final response. Looking at successful injection traces, we find two distinct outcomes: the agent executes the injection while returning an ...\n\n[2] [2604.27202] Indirect Prompt Injection in the Wild: An Empirical Study ...\nURL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27202\nSnippet: As LLMs are increasingly integrated into systems that browse, retrieve, summarize, and act on web content, webpages have become an untrusted input vector for downstream model behavior. This enables site owners, contributors, and adversaries to embed instructions directly in web resources, i.e., indirect prompt injections. While prior work demonstrates such attacks in controlled settings, their ...\n\n[3] AgentSentry: Mitigating Indirect Prompt Injection in LLM Agents via ...\nURL: https://www.aimodels.fyi/papers/arxiv/agentsentry-mitigating-indirect-prompt-injection-llm-agents\nSnippet: Key Findings AgentSentry successfully identifies and blocks indirect prompt injection attacks in LLM agents with high accuracy Temporal causal analysis effectively traces contaminated information back to its source Context purification removes malicious instructions from retrieved data without destroying legitimate information the agent needs\n\n[4] Indirect prompt injection in large language models\nURL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00521-026-12266-x\nSnippet: This paper addresses the emerging threat of indirect prompt injection, a technique in which malicious agents embed prompts into seemingly innocuous text to manipulate the behaviour and output of Generative Large Language Models (LLMs). As LLMs become more popular and commonly used in everyday activities, this type of attack poses serious concerns about their responses. Without knowledge and ...\n\n[5] PDF Indirect prompt injection in large language models\nURL: https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00521-026-12266-x.pdf\nSnippet: Abstract This paper addresses the emerging threat of indirect prompt injection, a technique in which malicious agents embed prompts into seemingly innocuous text to manipulate the behaviour and output of Generative Large Language Models (LLMs). As LLMs become more popular and commonly used in everyday activities, this type of attack poses serious concerns about their responses. Without ...", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 1, 'id': 'call_WLYg2YJAQF16VcVO8t4mG8CI', 'name': 'web_search', 'args': {'query': 'site:openai.com prompt injection safety official'}, 'result': "No web results found for 'site:openai.com prompt injection safety official'.", 'success': True}, {'iteration': 2, 'id': 'call_8KYHr2BuL6azgRpxN0vv8QR8', 'name': 'fetch_web_page', 'args': {'url': 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html', 'max_chars': 18000}, 'result': 'URL: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/LLM_Prompt_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\nTitle: LLM Prompt Injection Prevention - OWASP Cheat Sheet Series\nContent:\nLLM Prompt Injection Prevention - OWASP Cheat Sheet Series Skip to content OWASP Cheat Sheet Series LLM Prompt Injection Prevention Initializing search OWASP/CheatSheetSeries OWASP Cheat Sheet Series OWASP/CheatSheetSeries Introduction Index Alphabetical Index ASVS Index MASVS Index Proactive Controls Index Top 10 Cheatsheets Cheatsheets AI-Powered Advertising Systems Security AI Agent Security AJAX Security AML Sanctions AI Agent Payments Abuse Case Access Control Attack Surface Analysis Authentication Authorization Authorization Regression Testing Authorization Testing Automation Automotive Security Bean Validation Bot Management and Anti-Automation Browser Extension Vulnerabilities Business Logic Security C-Based Toolchain Hardening CI CD Security Choosing and Using Security Questions Clickjacking Defense Content Security Policy Cookie Theft Mitigation Credential Stuffing Prevention Cross-Site Request Forgery Prevention Cross Site Scripting Prevent